+++ b/docs/SEASONAL_RECOMMENDATION_ENGINE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,140 @@
+# AI-Powered Dynamic Seasonal Travel Recommendation Engine
+
+Resolves issue #773. Lets a visitor pick a **travel month** on the Travel
+page and see destinations ranked by weather suitability, festivals
+happening that month, and their own interests — with alternatives
+suggested when a destination isn't a good fit for that month.
+
+## Why rule-based, not a real ML model / paid weather API
+
+This project has no backend and no API keys (see `weather-service.js`'s
+use of the free, keyless Open-Meteo API for the existing Weather-Aware
+Itinerary feature, and `event-recommendation-engine.js`'s recurring
+month-window model for festivals, since most Indian festivals follow a
+lunar/regional calendar rather than a fixed Gregorian date). The seasonal
+engine follows the same approach: curated data + transparent scoring
+rules, run entirely client-side. This keeps recommendations reproducible,
+testable, and free to run, at the cost of being an approximation rather
+than a live forecast — the same tradeoff every other rule-based engine in
+this codebase already makes.
+
+## Architecture
+
+```
+js-modules/travel/
+├── travel-recommend-data.js        # TRAVEL_DESTINATIONS (issue #185, reused as-is)
+├── travel-recommend.js             # interest/budget/season preference engine (issue #185)
+├── seasonal-recommend-engine.js    # NEW — pure ranking logic (this feature)
+└── seasonal-recommend-ui.js        # NEW — DOM wiring for the "Seasonal Planner" section
+
+js-modules/event-data.js            # festival dataset (Smart Event Discovery feature, reused as-is)
+```
+
+`seasonal-recommend-engine.js` is a dependency-free, DOM-free module (same
+`(function(root){...})(window||globalThis)` + `module.exports` pattern as
+`travel-recommend.js` and `weather-core.js`) so it loads as a plain
+`<script>` in the browser and can also be `import`-ed directly in Vitest
+(`tests/unit/seasonal-recommend-engine.test.js`) without a DOM.
+
+It deliberately reuses the **existing** destination dataset
+(`TRAVEL_DESTINATIONS`) and festival dataset (`eventData.events`) instead
+of introducing a third, divergent data source — the only new "data" is a
+small set of scoring rules and a region→state lookup table, both defined
+in the engine file itself.
+
+## Ranking methodology
+
+For a requested month `m` (1–12):
+
+1. **Canonical season.** `m` maps to one of `winter` (Nov–Feb), `summer`
+   (Mar–Jun), or `monsoon` (Jul–Sep) via `MONTH_SEASON`.
+
+2. **Climate fit score (0–3).** Each destination already carries a
+   `seasons` tag (`winter` / `summer` / `monsoon` / `year-round`) from the
+   existing dataset:
+   - `year-round` → base score 2
+   - tagged season matches `m`'s canonical season → base score 3
+   - otherwise → base score 1
+
+   A small table of **category risk rules** (`RISK_RULES`) then subtracts
+   points when a destination's interest `tags` carry a known seasonal
+   hazard that isn't already reflected in its `seasons` tag — e.g.
+   `beaches`/`islands` destinations are downgraded in the monsoon
+   (cyclone/rough-sea risk), `wildlife` destinations are downgraded in the
+   monsoon (many parks close for the breeding season), and `desert`
+   destinations are downgraded in peak summer (extreme heat). The result
+   is clamped to 0–3 and mapped to a label:
+
+   | Score | Label |
+   |---|---|
+   | 3 | Peak season |
+   | 2 | Good time to visit |
+   | 1 | Off-season — possible but less ideal |
+   | 0 | Avoid — unfavorable conditions expected |
+
+3. **Festival bonus.** `getFestivalsForDestination` looks up
+   `eventData.events` whose recurring `[startMonth, endMonth]` window
+   covers `m` and whose `state` falls within the destination's `region`
+   (via the `REGION_STATES` lookup — an intentional approximation, since
+   regions span multiple states, matching the precision level the rest of
+   the festival dataset already uses). Each matching festival adds a flat
+   bonus to the ranking score and is surfaced by name in the explanation.
+
+4. **Preference score.** Same spirit as the existing preference engine
+   (`travel-recommend.js`): +2 per matched interest tag, +1 for a budget
+   match. Kept as a separate function (`scorePreferences`) rather than
+   reused directly, since that engine's own season-matching logic doesn't
+   apply here (a specific month replaces the coarse "any/winter/summer/
+   monsoon" preference).
+
+5. **Total score** = `climateScore × 2 + preferenceScore + (hasFestival ? 1.5 : 0)`.
+   Destinations scoring 0 (Avoid) are **excluded** from the primary
+   ranked list — they're only reachable through "See better-timed
+   alternatives".
+
+6. **Alternatives.** For any destination scoring ≤1 for the requested
+   month, `getAlternatives` finds other destinations sharing at least one
+   interest tag that score ≥2 that month, ranked by tag overlap then
+   climate score.
+
+7. **Explanations.** `buildSeasonalReason` composes a plain-language
+   sentence from the climate note, any matched festivals, and any matched
+   interests — shown under each result card as "💡 …".
+
+8. **Seasonal insights dashboard.** `getSeasonalInsights(destinations,
+   month, events)` tallies how many destinations are at peak / good /
+   off-season / avoid for the month, and lists festivals happening that
+   month, for the summary line above the results grid.
+
+## UI
+
+`seasonal-recommend-ui.js` renders a "Best Time to Visit — Seasonal
+Planner" section on `frontend/travel/travel.html` (below the existing
+"Recommended For You" section): a month chip-selector, optional interest
+chips, an insights summary line, and a results grid reusing the existing
+`.destination-card` / `.rec-chip` / `.rec-similar-pill` styling so it
+matches the rest of the Travel page.
+
+## Testing
+
+`tests/unit/seasonal-recommend-engine.test.js` covers month→season
+mapping, wrapping month ranges, climate scoring (including the risk-rule
+downgrades and year-round destinations), festival matching, full ranking
+(exclusion of "avoid" destinations, interest boosting, limits),
+alternative suggestions, and the insights dashboard. Run with:
+
+```
+npm run test:unit
+```
+
+## Limitations / future work
+
+- Climate fit is a static, curated rule set, not live historical weather
+  data — a genuine uplift here would mean sourcing IMD (India
+  Meteorological Department) climate normals per destination.
+- Festival matching is region-level, not per-city, so a festival in one
+  part of a region can appear against a destination elsewhere in the same
+  region.
+- No backend means no analytics on recommendation effectiveness (one of
+  the original issue's "Technical Considerations") — that would require a
+  server component this project doesn't currently have.
